{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.6,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["R67"],
      "pins": ["R67.1", "R67.2"],
      "nets": ["VOUTN", "VOUTP"],
      "observed": "R67 is valued 24k in the schematic and SPICE deck, but its library description and MPN both specify a 24Ω part (MPN FRC0805J240TSD, '24Ω 0805 Chip Resistor'). The value field and the part identity disagree by three orders of magnitude.",
      "expected": "R67's value should match its 24Ω part (24 ohm, 0805). A 24k value would be a different resistor from the one that is actually specified for this position.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(ref \"R67\") (value \"24k\")"},
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(field (name \"Description\") \" 125mW Thick Film Resistors 150V ±100ppm/℃ ±5% 24Ω 0805 Chip Resistor - Surface Mount ROHS\")"},
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(property (name \"MPN\") (value \"FRC0805J240TSD\"))"},
        {"source": "spice", "quote": "R67 VOUTN VOUTP 24k"}
      ],
      "impact": "If the board was populated with the 24Ω part, the value in the design is wrong. Across the VOUTP/VOUTN rail, a 24Ω element would load the output far more heavily than the 24k value intended, changing the current drawn from the supply and the behaviour of the output network.",
      "confidence": 0.6
    }
  ],
  "summary": "R67 is labelled 24k in the design, but its library description and MPN both identify a 24Ω part, so the value and the part identity disagree and one of them is wrong. I found no other clear connectivity, polarity or rating deviation in the supplied files."
}

